Sequential Axis-Tuning Effects in a Near-Field Optical Interference System
Overview

This repository documents preliminary observations from a simple near-field optical interference experiment involving:

Polarizing film
Printed periodic structures
Controlled XY rotation
Gradual Z-axis tilt adjustment

The experiments revealed strong dependence on the sequence of axis tuning.

In particular:

XY → Z tuning
tends to produce clearer circular structures and surrounding satellite/moiré-like patterns.
Z → XY tuning
mainly produces linear fringe structures with weak or unstable moiré behavior.

These observations suggest that the system may possess genuine 3D directional sensitivity and path-dependent optical state behavior.

Key Observation

The emergence of moiré/satellite structures is not determined solely by static geometry.

Instead, the order of rotational and tilt adjustments appears to influence the resulting coherent pattern state.

Observed behavior:

Tuning Sequence	Dominant Pattern
XY → Z	Circular sharpening + satellite/moiré emergence
Z → XY	Linear fringes, weak/transient moiré
Experimental Notes
Camera autofocus disabled
Exposure fixed
Hand motion minimized
Polarizing film manually rotated
Small angular changes produce significant pattern transitions

The observed structures are often extremely small and may require careful frame-by-frame inspection.

Current Interpretation

The behavior may involve combined effects of:

Near-field interference
Polarization-dependent phase modulation
Angular anisotropy
Multi-axis coherence coupling
Direction-sensitive phase evolution

At present, no claim is made regarding chirality or circular dichroism, although directional asymmetry remains an open question for future investigation.

Status

This is an early exploratory experiment and should be considered qualitative rather than quantitative.

The purpose of this repository is to document reproducible visual behavior and encourage further analysis.

